---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { guideBySlug } from '../../lib/guides.ts';
const guide = guideBySlug('jsonl-ndjson-viewer-validator');
const sample = '{"event":"started","id":1}\n{"event":"finished","id":1,"durationMs":142}\n{"event":"started","id":2,"ok":true}';
---

<GuideLayout guide={guide} keywords="JSONL viewer, NDJSON validator, JSON Lines parser, JSONL to JSON array, invalid JSONL line, newline delimited JSON logs">
  <p>JSONL, JSON Lines, and NDJSON are names for the same useful convention: each non-empty line is its own complete JSON value. There is no surrounding array. That small difference makes the format friendly to streaming logs and large exports, but it also means a normal JSON parser cannot read the whole file at once.</p>

  <h2>Recognize a JSON Lines file</h2>
  <p>This is three JSON records, not one JSON array:</p>
  <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre>
  <p>Wrapping those values in <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> without adding commas would produce invalid JSON. Use the <a href="/jsonl-viewer/">JSONL Viewer &amp; NDJSON Validator</a> to parse each line independently and inspect the records that succeed.</p>

  <h2>Find a bad line without discarding good records</h2>
  <p>A trailing comma, a truncated line, an unescaped quote, or an accidental log prefix can break a single record. The viewer reports the actual line number and preserves valid records in its preview. Filter the visible JSON when an export is noisy, then select a record to see a formatted value instead of scanning a long one-line payload.</p>

  <h2>Convert valid JSON Lines to a JSON array</h2>
  <p>After you decide how to handle invalid records, download the valid values as a conventional JSON array. That format can move into the <a href="/json-formatter/">JSON Formatter</a>, a fixture file, or the <a href="/json-to-code/">JSON-to-code generator</a>. Exporting valid records is deliberately explicit so a bad line is never silently treated as if it were good data.</p>

  <h2>Share a minimal safe reproducer</h2>
  <p>The viewer’s share link restores the input, filter, and parse action. Use it for a tiny synthetic example when asking why an ingestion pipeline rejects a record. Links can be read by anyone who receives them, so redact log messages, access tokens, customer data, internal hosts, and identifiers before sharing.</p>

